## Flexbox vs CSS Grid

## Flexbox — One Dimension

```css
.nav {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;      /* cross axis */
  justify-content: space-between; /* main axis */
  gap: 1rem;
}

/* Center anything */
.card {
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
}
```

Best for: navbars, button groups, card footers, centering, any single-row/column layout.

## CSS Grid — Two Dimensions

```css
.page-layout {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 240px 1fr;
  grid-template-rows: auto 1fr auto;
  grid-template-areas:
    'sidebar header'
    'sidebar main'
    'sidebar footer';
  min-height: 100vh;
}

.sidebar { grid-area: sidebar; }
.main    { grid-area: main; }

/* Auto-fill responsive grid */
.card-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(280px, 1fr));
  gap: 1.5rem;
}
```

Best for: page layouts, dashboards, magazine-style grids, any two-dimensional placement.

## Rule of Thumb

> **Grid for layout, Flexbox for components.**

They are not mutually exclusive — a Grid item can itself be a Flex container.

