## CSS Grid Systems

## Traditional (Framework) Grid

Bootstrap-style grids used a 12-column float-based system with responsive utility classes:

```html
<div class="row">
  <div class="col-md-8">Main</div>
  <div class="col-md-4">Sidebar</div>
</div>
```

The 12-column convention is arbitrary but divisible by 2, 3, 4, and 6 — making it flexible for most layouts.

## Native CSS Grid

```css
/* 12-column grid */
.grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(12, 1fr);
  gap: 1.5rem;
}

.main    { grid-column: span 8; }
.sidebar { grid-column: span 4; }

/* Responsive — no media query needed */
.card-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(300px, 1fr));
  gap: 1rem;
}
```

## Named Grid Lines

```css
.layout {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns:
    [full-start] 1rem
    [content-start] 1fr
    [content-end] 1rem
    [full-end];
}

.breakout { grid-column: full; }    /* edge-to-edge */
.article  { grid-column: content; } /* contained */
```

Native CSS Grid eliminates the need for framework grid classes and works without JavaScript or a build step.

