## CSS Sprites

A sprite sheet combines many images into one file. Each image is revealed by setting `background-position` to offset into the correct region.

## Implementation

```css
/* Shared class for all icons using the sprite */
.icon {
  display: inline-block;
  background-image: url('/images/sprite.png');
  background-repeat: no-repeat;
}

/* Each icon reveals its slice */
.icon--home   { width: 24px; height: 24px; background-position: 0 0; }
.icon--search { width: 24px; height: 24px; background-position: -24px 0; }
.icon--user   { width: 24px; height: 24px; background-position: -48px 0; }
```

## Why Sprites Were Used

HTTP/1.1 was limited to ~6 connections per domain. Each image = one request. A 50-icon set meant 50 round-trips. Sprites reduced that to one.

## Why They're Largely Obsolete

- **HTTP/2** multiplexes many requests over one connection — small individual files are cheap.
- **SVG sprites** — inline `<svg>` with `<use>` references a `<symbol>` by ID, no background hacks needed.
- **Icon fonts** (Font Awesome) — scalable, colorable via `color` property.

```html
<!-- SVG sprite (modern approach) -->
<svg style="display:none">
  <symbol id="icon-home" viewBox="0 0 24 24">...</symbol>
</svg>

<svg aria-hidden="true"><use href="#icon-home" /></svg>
```

Raster sprites are still worth considering for pixel-art games or when dozens of small PNGs must load at once.

